Only the interval members are centred, and only the boundary-conditioned one is calibrated, everywhere on the design plane

Figures 2 and 3 measure one recording condition. Figure 4 asks what changes over the plane. Ten thousand recordings are simulated at every cell of a grid in the sampling interval, the instrumental noise and the channel count, and scored by the same eight members, the recursive family in figure supplement 3.

The verdict does not change. Across the plane INR and IR miss the channel number by one and two per cent, where the other four macroscopic members miss it by a fifth to a third, and IR is the only member whose error bar is honest. They all miss in the same direction: nine in ten of the cells outside the band report an error bar too narrow rather than too wide. Where they fail, the four that leave the correlation unmodelled report bars two to three times too narrow, and R and IR about a tenth. Neither partial correction improves on the member it corrects (figure supplement 3).

What the plane adds is where each verdict breaks. IR's closing-rate error bar is honest everywhere except at the two smallest channel counts with the least noise, and it is the channel count that repairs it: its closing-rate distortion converges from 1.37 at ten channels to 1.006 at ten thousand, as both the current and the state distribution become Gaussian by the central limit.

Instrumental noise repairs everyone. As it grows the recorded current becomes Gaussian whatever the member assumes: the closing-rate distortion of least squares falls from 13 to 1 as the noise rises by five decades, and R covers the same ground in two. Colour at the noisy end of each row should not be read cell by cell: ten thousand recordings often cannot resolve a ten per cent bias there, and the variation between neighbours is sampling noise. 

A short sampling interval attenuates the missing average without removing it. The channel-number bias of NR falls by a factor of forty from the coarsest sampling to the finest and R's by sixteen, but at the finest they still carry seven and four per cent where INR and IR carry under one. The two least-squares arms part only at the coarsest interval, where the arm that does not average picks up a rate bias close to twenty times the other's (figure supplement 2); that single flag is why both are on the grid.

Two qualifications. The anchor was checked against a differenced Fisher information and overturns no verdict, least squares being the one it would make more conservative (Supplementary File 1). And at the highest channel count with the coarsest interval the amplitude directions of R are not identified; those cells are drawn grey.
